WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.310
This consumer takes payments from a queue.

2
00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.299
If the queue is empty, it waits.

3
00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:09.909
When it wakes up, it takes the first item.

4
00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:12.165
With one consumer, this works.

5
00:00:12.165 --> 00:00:15.279
With two, it can throw NoSuchElementException.

6
00:00:15.279 --> 00:00:17.513
To see why, look inside the lock.

7
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:20.898
Every Java object has a monitor.

8
00:00:20.898 --> 00:00:25.701
One thread at a time owns it: the thread inside the synchronized block.

9
00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:29.004
Threads that want the lock wait in the entry set.

10
00:00:29.004 --> 00:00:30.320
They show as BLOCKED.

11
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:33.596
Threads that called wait sit in the wait set.

12
00:00:33.596 --> 00:00:36.115
They show as WAITING, and use no CPU.

13
00:00:36.933 --> 00:00:40.343
Consumer 1 takes the lock and checks the queue.

14
00:00:40.343 --> 00:00:41.213
It is empty.

15
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:42.488
So it calls wait.

16
00:00:42.488 --> 00:00:46.396
wait gives back the lock, and parks the thread in the wait set.

17
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.287
Consumer 2 does the same.

18
00:00:48.287 --> 00:00:50.987
It finds the queue empty, and waits too.

19
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.625
Now a producer takes the lock, adds one payment to the queue, and calls notifyAll.

20
00:00:56.833 --> 00:01:00.914
notifyAll wakes every waiter, but it does not hand over the lock.

21
00:01:00.914 --> 00:01:05.999
Both consumers move to the entry set, and compete for the lock like any newcomer.

22
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.257
When the producer leaves its block, the lock is free.

23
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:11.105
Consumer 1 gets it first.

24
00:01:11.105 --> 00:01:14.643
Its wait returns, it takes the payment, and it leaves.

25
00:01:14.867 --> 00:01:18.326
Then consumer 2 gets the lock, and its wait returns too.

26
00:01:18.326 --> 00:01:21.537
But the if checked the queue once, before it waited.

27
00:01:21.537 --> 00:01:24.935
The next line takes the first item from an empty queue.

28
00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:27.498
The payment it was woken for is gone.

29
00:01:27.498 --> 00:01:29.767
Another consumer got the lock first.

30
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.956
Now write while instead of if, and replay the moment consumer 2 gets the lock.

31
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:37.164
wait returns inside the loop.

32
00:01:37.164 --> 00:01:39.642
So consumer 2 tests the queue again.

33
00:01:39.642 --> 00:01:44.187
It is empty, so it calls wait again and goes back to the wait set.

34
00:01:44.187 --> 00:01:45.220
Nothing breaks.

35
00:01:45.433 --> 00:01:47.099
There is a second reason.

36
00:01:47.099 --> 00:01:54.093
The Javadoc for Object.wait says a thread can wake up without being notified, interrupted, or timing out.

37
00:01:54.093 --> 00:01:56.291
That is called a spurious wakeup.

38
00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.410
It is rare, but it is allowed.

39
00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:02.295
The loop handles it the same way: test again, and wait again.

40
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:09.195
That is all a guarded block is: a while loop around wait, with the condition as the loop test.

41
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.267
Every wait sits inside a while loop that tests the condition.

42
00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:17.640
Treat an if around wait as a bug, even in code that has never failed.

43
00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.556
It has just never had two waiters at once yet.
